# mboss superproject — local docker compose
# environment. Copy to .env and adjust. .env is
# gitignored; this file is committed.
#
# Almost nothing lives here any more. Compose
# reads each app service's environment from its
# own submodule's file:
#
#   web   ← mboss-web/.env.local
#   api   ← mboss-nodejs-api/.env
#   dbos  ← mboss-nodejs-dbos/.env
#
# Each has a committed .env.example beside it to
# copy, and each is where you change a value.
# Setting one here does nothing at all:
# docker-compose.yml no longer names those
# variables, and a root .env only feeds ${…}
# interpolation in that file — it never reaches a
# container on its own. Nor should compose start
# naming them again, because its environment:
# always beats its env_file:, so any key it
# carries is a key the submodule file can no
# longer change.
#
# All three must be populated before web, api and
# dbos will work — not merely before they start.
# api and dbos refuse to boot without theirs, but
# web reads its environment on first use: with
# the file missing it comes up healthy and
# answers /healthz, then 500s on the first
# request that needs a value. A green `docker
# compose ps` is not evidence that
# mboss-web/.env.local is complete. `docker
# compose up -d postgres` works without any of
# them.
#
# Postgres's own credentials are not read from
# here either. They are literals in
# docker-compose.yml, because no submodule owns
# them and nothing outside this stack sets them.

# The one value this file still drives. It is
# the origin every manage and unsubscribe link
# in every email is built from, and
# docker-compose.yml overrides
# mboss-nodejs-dbos/.env with it deliberately:
# that file holds the production origin, which
# is right for a worker run on the host and
# useless in a link you want to click locally.
# Set this if the stack runs anywhere other
# than :3000, or the links in the mail point at
# a port nothing is listening on.
SITE_URL=http://localhost:3000
